From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 18 Sep 1994 09:29:23 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-235] Omega Point

Does anybody have an in depth critique of Frank Tipler's 
_The Physics of Immortality_, from the point of view of
either physics or computer science?  So far I've only skimmed it,
and read his _Omni_ interview.  I'm not sure it's worthwhile
reading more; however if it becomes influential enough to
rival Extropianism as a theory of scientific immortality,
then it's worth tackling in depth.  From what I understand from
the bit I've read, he claims to prove three theses:

	* the existance of God
	* human free will
	* resurrection of the soul 

Perhaps the most interesting, falsifiable part, from my
viewpoint as a computer scientist interested in long lifespans,
is the claim of resurrection.  I understand the argument to
combine physics and computer science: that computational power 
will approach infinity as the universe collapses.  This 
doesn't jibe with what I understand about thermodynamics;
Freeman Dyson has argued that computation grows more efficient
as the environment grows colder.  But I'm no physicist.

As well, Frank Tipler is no computer scientist.  I wonder what 
will be the growth rate in historical emulation versus the growth 
rate in new, "godlike" forms of computation?  How many cycles 
will be spent in _recreating_ life histories versus _extending_ 
them?  Call me Extropian, but I'd rather buy and sell the 
god cycles than get doled out infinitesimal fractions of 
the cycles that might be devoted to enslaved historical recreations.  
And if there's any "heaven" or "hell" to be run, I'd rather
have my Saint Peter agent reading names off my "who shall
we resurrect today, kids?" list, than be some helpless sheep or goat 
on the list of some pyromaniac Omegan sheep-herding diety.  Although
by Tipler's theory maybe I'll end up being all those kinds
of beings and more, in tiny little gradations.

Also fascinating is that while Tipler claims to be making a
pro religion argument, in fact he is presenting (to the extent 
his argument holds) a strong argument in favor of unbundling,
not just cosmology from religion, but the very concepts
of God, soul, and immortality from religion.  The fundamental
role of religion is not any of those ideas; rather it has
used those ideas to propagate traditional morality. (cf.
for example, Hayek's excellent comments on religion). 

If the Omega dieties bear no resemblance to us whatsoever,
then the Omega Point has no necessary or predictable connection 
to our present beliefs or behavior.  If the Omegan dieties are 
like us at all, they may well choose who to emulate for their 
entertainment value.  They might decide Hitler and Stalin would 
cause far more commotion and drama in their little emulations 
than boring characters like Bishop Tutu and Mother Theresa.  Perhaps 
to win Pascal's Wager one must strive, not to live the good life, 
but rather to live a life that will achieve the highest Omega Nielson 
ratings.  Tipler has, therefore, rebutted Dan Quayle.  
Hollywood is not corrupting the values of America; quite the 
opposite -- it's  instilling America with the very values we will
need to achieve Omegan immortality.  Not just fifteen
minutes of fame, but rather infinite amounts of fame,
using infinite cycles compressed into infinitesimal time
slices, after we've waited an infinite amount of subjective
time for the Omegan Singularity.  Or something like that.

Alas, I find immortality that covers only an infinitsemimal 
fraction of the future, to be only infinitesimally useful.  
I guess it's back to those boring, banal, everyday technologies 
of immortality, such as cryonics and uploading.

Rapture of the Omega Point Can Be Cured,
Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

