From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Sun, 18 Sep 1994 13:29:49 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-239] Liquid Sentience

 Read the interview with Mark Miller in Extropy #10, where he
describes his credit theory of personal identity. He predicts
that the communication bandwidth between brains will become as
high as the bandwidth within the brain. He suggests a definition
of identity based on how much responsibility a prior entity had
for the memes that make the current entity what they are. "It's
really a shame the Greek civilization died." "They haven't died;
we're it."

 I also recommend reading Poul Anderson's _The Rebel Worlds_.
There is an intelligent race in that story which has evolved as
a symbiosis between 3 different species. Each symbiont by itself
is a rather dumb animal, but when they assemble themselves so that
the nervous systems of 3 symbionts are joined, a complete personality
emerges. A single symbiont may be a part of several different
personalities, only one of which can be assembled at any one time.
 It would be interesting to define a legal system that works well
for such a society. For instance suppose that 2 symbionts force an
unwilling 3rd symbiont to join into a personality with them. The
victim of this crime may not be intelligent enough to own property
by itself - who should get restitution? Is the victim responsible
for the actions of the personality it has been forced to join (suppose
it can't prove it was unwilling to be assembled into that personality)?

 I think this provides some hints about how future intelligence will be
organized, which will include a bunch of semi-intelligent software agents
being assembled as needed into intelligent personas.

